TOLL GATE SHIFT CLOSER

Toll Gate Shift Closer — a real-world task using only today's concepts: while loops, break and continue, counting with variables, comparisons, if / elif / else, variables, input(), basic arithmetic, and printing with 2-decimal money format. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A toll gate on the Port Harcourt–Aba expressway needs a small program for the cashier's desk. The operator logs every vehicle as it passes. Cars pay N500, buses pay N1000. Official vehicles (ambulance, fire service, government convoy) pass free - they count as vehicles passed but bring in no money. When the shift ends, the operator types close and the day's summary prints.

The program keeps asking one question forever until close is typed:

Vehicle (car / bus / official / close):

Rules:
- car: add N500 to the revenue and count the vehicle.
- bus: add N1000 to the revenue and count the vehicle.
- official: print "Official vehicle passed free", count the vehicle, but add nothing to revenue - and go straight back to the question.
- close: print the summary and stop the program.
- anything else: print "Unknown vehicle type", count nothing, and go straight back to the question.

The summary is exactly three lines:

VEHICLES PASSED: 4
OFFICIAL VEHICLES: 1
REVENUE: N2000.00

Sample run:

Vehicle (car / bus / official / close): car
Vehicle (car / bus / official / close): official
Official vehicle passed free
Vehicle (car / bus / official / close): bus
Vehicle (car / bus / official / close): car
Vehicle (car / bus / official / close): close

VEHICLES PASSED: 4
OFFICIAL VEHICLES: 1
REVENUE: N2000.00

Edge cases to handle: close typed as the very first entry (zero vehicles, N0.00 - must not crash), official as the very first entry, an unknown type like truck in the middle of a shift (message printed, nothing counted, the loop continues), a shift with only official vehicles (revenue N0.00 but vehicles counted), and a long shift mixing all four entries.
